<div class="tale-introduction"><p>From the <strong>Mahabharata, Vana Parva, Nalopakhyana Parva</strong>. The sage Brihadashva tells this account to Yudhishthira during the Pandavas' exile.</p></div>
<section class="story-characters" aria-labelledby="characters-title"><p class="eyebrow">MEET THE CHARACTERS</p><h2 id="characters-title">Characters in this story</h2><dl><div><dt>Nala / Bahuka</dt><dd>King of Nishadha, famed for his skill with horses and later disguised as the charioteer Bahuka.</dd></div><div><dt>Damayanti</dt><dd>Princess of Vidarbha, daughter of King Bhima, whose intelligence and perseverance guide her search.</dd></div><div><dt>Pushkara</dt><dd>Nala's brother and opponent in the disastrous game of dice.</dd></div><div><dt>Kali</dt><dd>A personified force of discord in the narrative who takes advantage of Nala's lapse and weakness.</dd></div><div><dt>Karkotaka</dt><dd>The serpent rescued by Nala who changes his appearance and aids his eventual recovery.</dd></div><div><dt>Rituparna</dt><dd>King of Ayodhya, who employs Bahuka and exchanges the science of dice for skill with horses.</dd></div><div><dt>Brihadashva and Yudhishthira</dt><dd>The sage narrating the account and the king to whom it offers consolation.</dd></div></dl></section>
